-
-**WHAT IT DOES**
+    <help format="markdown"><![CDATA[
+**What it does**
 
-Hash BAM and FASTQ files to verify data integrity
+Hash BAM, CRAM and FASTQ files to verify data integrity.
 
-For each pair of reads in a BAM or FASTQ file we compute a hash value composed of the readname,
-whether it is first or last in pair, sequence and quality value. All the hash values are summed
-up so the result is independent of the ordering within the files. The result can be compared to
-verify that the pair of FASTQ files contain the same read information as the aligned BAM file.
+For each pair of reads in a BAM/CRAM or FASTQ file a hash value is computed, composed of the
+readname, whether it is first or last in pair, sequence and quality value. All the hash values
+are summed up so the result is independent of the ordering within the files. The result can be
+compared to verify that a pair of FASTQ files contains the same read information as the aligned
+BAM/CRAM file.
 
 -----
 
-**BAM**
+**BAM / CRAM**
+
+Processes a number of BAM or CRAM files. Files are assumed to contain paired end reads. If you
+run with `--no-paired` all reads are treated as single end and a warning is displayed if any
+read is marked as "second in pair" in the file.
 
-processes a number of BAM files. BAM files are assumed to contain paired end reads.
-If you run with --no-paired it treats all reads as single end and displays a warning if any read is marked as "second in pair" in the BAM file.
+Each read must carry an `RG` (read group) tag; the checksum is reported per read group as
+`readgroup`, `checksum` and `readcount` (tab separated), one line per read group found in the
+file header.
+
+For CRAM files that do not store the reference in their header, supply the reference FASTA file
+so the reads can be decoded.
 
 -----
 
 **FASTA**
 
-processes a number of FASTA files. All FASTA files are assumed to be single end reads with no quality information.
-To compare to a BAM file, run bamhash_checksum_bam --no-paired --no-quality
+Processes a number of FASTA files. All FASTA files are assumed to be single end reads with no
+quality information. To compare to a BAM file, run BamHash on the BAM with `--no-paired
+--no-quality`.
 
 -----
 
-
 **FASTQ**
 
-processes a number of FASTQ files. FASTQ files are assumed to contain paired end reads,
-such that the first two files contain the first pair of reads, etc. If any of the read names in the two pairs don't match the program exits with failure.
-
+Processes a number of FASTQ files. FASTQ files are assumed to contain paired end reads, such
+that the first two files contain the first pair of reads, etc. If any of the read names in a
+pair don't match, the program exits with failure.
 
 -----
 
